<h3>Section 3: Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>Activity A. The Mirror of Dignity</h4>
<p>Stand in front of a mirror and look into your own eyes. Say aloud: &laquo;You are the image of God. God loves you. You have infinite dignity.&raquo; Do this every day this week (even if it feels awkward at first). After seven days, write here how it has affected you:</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>Activity B. The Portrait of Dignity</h4>
<p>Choose a person you find difficult to treat with respect (a difficult coworker, a conflicting family member, someone with different opinions). Write three things that God &mdash; who sees beyond appearances &mdash; would see in them that you do not see:</p>
<p>1. <span style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:0 60px">&nbsp;</span></p>
<p>2. <span style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:0 60px">&nbsp;</span></p>
<p>3. <span style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);padding:0 60px">&nbsp;</span></p>

<h4>Activity C. The Dignity Detector</h4>
<p>This week, every time you encounter another person &mdash; especially someone you normally ignore (the bus driver, the cleaning staff, the person begging on the street) &mdash; say silently to yourself: &laquo;Image of God.&raquo; At the end of the week, write what changed in you:</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>Activity D. A Concrete Action</h4>
<p>Do one concrete thing this week that honors your dignity as a child of God. Choose one:</p>
<ul>
<li>Take good care of your body (exercise, rest, nourishing food)</li>
<li>Dedicate real time to silent prayer</li>
<li>Say no to something that degrades you (a habit, a website, a conversation)</li>
<li>Help someone in need without wanting anything in return</li>
<li>Write a letter to one person thanking them for something</li>
</ul>
<p>Which one did you choose? What happened when you did it?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>Activity E. Saint Connection: St. Josephine Bakhita</h4>
<p>Read about St. Josephine Bakhita (search online or read her brief biography). She was kidnapped, enslaved, tortured, branded &mdash; and yet said: &laquo;I am definitively loved.&raquo;</p>
<p>Answer: What does her story teach you about where dignity really comes from? How does it help you understand that dignity is indestructible?</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>

<h4>Activity F. &laquo;Wonder&raquo; Connection</h4>
<p>If possible, watch the film <em>Wonder</em> (2017) this week &mdash; or at minimum watch the trailer. Auggie Pullman has a severe facial deformity. In what specific moments of the film (or from what you imagine) does his dignity shine through despite how others treat him? What does &laquo;choose kind&raquo; have to do with human dignity?</p>
